    {
      "card_id": "card_22_spherical_mirror_intro",
      "card_slug": "card_22_spherical_mirror_intro",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "spherical_mirrors",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 22,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Raju, tumne kabhi car ka side mirror dekha? Ya dentist ke paas wo chhota round mirror?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Haan Sir! Car wala mirror chota image dikhata hai aur dentist wala bada!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Perfect observation! Ye curved mirrors hain, specifically Spherical Mirrors - jinka reflecting surface sphere ka hissa hota hai. Do types hain - Concave (andar ki taraf curved, like dentist mirror) aur Convex (bahar ki taraf curved, like car side mirror)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh dono ka kaam alag hai?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_23_concave_vs_convex",
        "button_text": "Difference Samjho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_23_concave_vs_convex",
      "card_slug": "card_23_concave_vs_convex",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "concave_convex_difference",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 23,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Haan! Concave mirror (converging mirror) - parallel rays ko ek point pe focus karta hai. Convex mirror (diverging mirror) - parallel rays ko spread (diverge) karta hai. Isliye concave se magnified (bada) image ban sakta hai, convex hamesha diminished (chhota) image banata hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Matlab concave mein bada dekhne ko milta hai isliye shaving mirror, torch, makeup mirror mein use hota hai?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Bilkul sahi! Aur convex mirror ka wider field of view hota hai (zyada area dikhta hai) isliye rear-view mirror, street corners, shops mein security mirror mein use hota hai! ⭐ BPSC/SSC Favorite - Application-based matching questions!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Sir, spherical mirror mein kuch important points hote hain na - pole, focus, centre of curvature?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_24_mirror_terminology",
        "button_text": "Terminology Seekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_24_mirror_terminology",
      "card_slug": "card_24_mirror_terminology",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "mirror_terminology",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 24,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Ekdum! POLE (P) - mirror ka centre point. CENTRE OF CURVATURE (C) - jis sphere ka mirror part hai uska centre. RADIUS OF CURVATURE (R) - PC distance. PRINCIPAL AXIS - pole se centre of curvature tak seedhi line. FOCUS (F) - jahan parallel rays converge/appear to diverge. FOCAL LENGTH (f) - PF distance."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Itne saare terms! Sir, focus aur centre of curvature mein kya relation hai?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Golden relation yaad rakho - Focal Length = Half of Radius of Curvature. Matlab f = R/2 ya R = 2f. Focus hamesha pole aur centre of curvature ke beech mein hota hai! ⭐ NEET/BPSC Formula Favorite!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Achhaaaa... toh agar radius 20 cm hai toh focal length 10 cm hogi!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_25_sign_convention",
        "button_text": "Sign Convention ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_25_sign_convention",
      "card_slug": "card_25_sign_convention",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "sign_convention",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 25,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Perfect! Ab bahut important - New Cartesian Sign Convention: (1) Pole (P) origin hai. (2) Principal axis ke LEFT se RIGHT ko positive direction maana jata hai. (3) Heights measured UPWARD = positive, DOWNWARD = negative. (4) Distances measured OPPOSITE to incident light = negative, ALONG incident light = positive."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Ye toh confusing lag raha hai Sir!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Ruko, simple trick - Object hamesha LEFT side pe hai (light left se aati hai). Toh object distance (u) HAMESHA NEGATIVE hoga! Concave mirror ka focus LEFT side (front) mein hai toh focal length (f) NEGATIVE. Convex ka focus RIGHT side (behind) mein hai toh f POSITIVE! ⭐ NEET/BPSC Trap - Sign convention galat lagane se answer wrong!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh concave mein f negative, convex mein f positive yaad rakhna hai!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_26_mirror_formula",
        "button_text": "Formula Dekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_26_mirror_formula",
      "card_slug": "card_26_mirror_formula",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "mirror_formula",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 26,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Ab Officer-level formula - MIRROR FORMULA: 1/f = 1/v + 1/u. Jahan f = focal length, v = image distance (from pole), u = object distance (from pole). Aur MAGNIFICATION formula: m = -v/u = height of image/height of object."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Sir, magnification negative ya positive kaise pata karein?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Agar m negative hai toh image INVERTED (ulta) hai aur REAL hai. Agar m positive hai toh image ERECT (sidha) hai aur VIRTUAL hai. Agar |m| > 1 toh magnified, |m| < 1 toh diminished, |m| = 1 toh same size! ⭐ NEET High-Weightage Concept!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Wow! Sign se hi sab pata chal jata hai!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_27_concave_image_positions",
        "button_text": "Image Positions ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_27_concave_image_positions",
      "card_slug": "card_27_concave_image_positions",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "concave_image_cases",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 27,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Concave mirror mein object ki position change karne se image properties change hoti hain. KEY CASES: (1) Object at INFINITY → Image at F, highly diminished, real, inverted. (2) Object BEYOND C → Image between F and C, diminished, real, inverted. (3) Object AT C → Image at C, same size, real, inverted."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Aur agar object focus pe rakhe toh?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "(4) Object AT F → Image at INFINITY, highly magnified, real, inverted. (5) Object BETWEEN F and P → Image BEHIND mirror, magnified, virtual, erect (dentist mirror case!). ⭐ BPSC/NEET Favorite - Tabular matching questions on object-image positions!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Aur convex mirror mein?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_28_convex_image",
        "button_text": "Convex Samjho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_28_convex_image",
      "card_slug": "card_28_convex_image",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "convex_image",
      "level": 3,
      "progress": {
        "current": 28,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Convex mirror bahut simple hai! Object ki position kuch bhi ho, image HAMESHA: (1) Behind the mirror (virtual), (2) Between P and F, (3) Erect, (4) Diminished (smaller than object). Isliye wider field of view milta hai - perfect for rear-view mirrors!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Matlab convex mein kabhi bhi real image nahi ban sakta?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Ekdum correct! Convex mirror NEVER forms a real image - always virtual, erect, diminished. ⭐ BPSC TRE/SSC Statement Trap - 'Convex mirror can form real image' is ALWAYS FALSE! Car mirrors pe likha hota hai 'Objects in mirror are closer than they appear' kyunki diminished image door ka illusion deta hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Ye toh practical bhi hai aur exam bhi!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
