    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_47_lens_intro",
      "card_slug": "card_47_lens_intro",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "lens_intro",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 47,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Raju, tumhari Mummy cooking mein chasma lagati hain? Ya Dadu ko newspaper padhne ke liye chasma chahiye?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Haan Sir! Dadu ko reading glasses chahiye aur Mummy ka chasma alag hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Perfect! Ye sab LENSES hain. Lens ek transparent optical device hai made of glass/plastic with at least one curved surface. Lenses refraction principle pe kaam karte hain - light ko bend karke ek point pe focus ya diverge karte hain!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh lens ka kaam light ko focus karna hai?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_48_lens_types",
        "button_text": "Types Dekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_48_lens_types",
      "card_slug": "card_48_lens_types",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "lens_types",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 48,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Do main types hain: CONVEX LENS (Converging Lens) - beech mein mota, edges thin. Parallel rays ko CONVERGE (focus) karta hai. CONCAVE LENS (Diverging Lens) - beech mein patla, edges thick. Parallel rays ko DIVERGE (spread) karta hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Matlab convex lens se magnifying glass banta hai?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Bilkul! Convex lens applications: Camera, human eye, magnifying glass, microscope, telescope, projector. Concave lens: Spectacles for myopia (nearsightedness), peepholes, car headlights (with reflector). ⭐ SSC/BPSC Application-Based Matching Favorite!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Sir lens mein bhi pole, focus, centre of curvature hote hain?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_49_lens_terminology",
        "button_text": "Terminology Seekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_49_lens_terminology",
      "card_slug": "card_49_lens_terminology",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "lens_terminology",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 49,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Haan! OPTICAL CENTRE (O) - lens ka central point. PRINCIPAL AXIS - optical centre se perpendicular axis. PRINCIPAL FOCUS (F) - Convex mein: parallel rays converge hote hain yahan. Concave mein: parallel rays appear to diverge from yahan. FOCAL LENGTH (f) - O to F distance. Lens ke DO foci hote hain - dono taraf!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Mirror mein ek focus tha, lens mein do?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Haan! Kyunki light lens ke dono side se aa sakti hai. Aur both foci optical centre se equal distance pe hote hain. APERTURE - lens ka effective diameter. ⭐ NEET Favorite - Lens has TWO foci (one on each side), mirror has ONE focus!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh lens ka sign convention?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_50_lens_sign_convention",
        "button_text": "Sign Convention ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_50_lens_sign_convention",
      "card_slug": "card_50_lens_sign_convention",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "lens_sign_convention",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 50,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Same as mirror - New Cartesian Sign Convention! Optical centre = origin. LEFT to RIGHT = positive direction. Object distance (u) always NEGATIVE (object left side pe). Convex lens ka focus RIGHT side (opposite to object) = POSITIVE f. Concave lens ka focus LEFT side (same side as object) = NEGATIVE f!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Matlab convex lens f positive, concave lens f negative - mirror ke opposite!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Exactly! ⭐ BPSC/NEET Major Trap - Convex MIRROR f positive but Convex LENS bhi f positive. Concave MIRROR f negative but Concave LENS bhi f negative! Don't confuse mirror-lens signs!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Lens ka formula bhi same hoga?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_51_lens_formula",
        "button_text": "Formula Dekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_51_lens_formula",
      "card_slug": "card_51_lens_formula",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "lens_formula",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 51,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Haan! LENS FORMULA: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u (dhyan do, mirror mein + tha, lens mein - hai!). MAGNIFICATION: m = v/u = height of image / height of object. Agar m negative = real, inverted. Agar m positive = virtual, erect. |m| > 1 = magnified!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh formula almost same hai bas sign change hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Correct! ⭐ NEET Formula Trap - 1/f = 1/v + 1/u (MIRROR), 1/f = 1/v - 1/u (LENS). Confusion se wrong answer! Aur ek important term - POWER of Lens: P = 1/f (focal length in meters). Unit: Dioptre (D). Convex lens P positive, Concave lens P negative!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Power ka matlab?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_52_power_of_lens",
        "button_text": "Power Samjho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_52_power_of_lens",
      "card_slug": "card_52_power_of_lens",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "power_lens",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 52,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Power of lens uski converging/diverging ability ko measure karta hai. Jitni kam focal length, utna zyada power - zyada bending! Formula: P = 1/f (f in meters). Agar f = 0.5 m (50 cm convex lens), toh P = 1/0.5 = +2 D. Agar f = -0.25 m (25 cm concave lens), toh P = 1/(-0.25) = -4 D."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh doctor jab +2.5 D ka chasma likhte hain toh convex lens hai!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Perfect! +2.5 D means convex lens with f = 1/2.5 = 0.4 m = 40 cm. Used for HYPERMETROPIA (farsightedness - door dikhta hai, paas nahi). -3 D means concave lens for MYOPIA (nearsightedness - paas dikhta hai, door nahi). ⭐ BPSC/SSC Application Favorite!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Sir convex lens mein image position cases batayein!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_53_convex_lens_cases",
        "button_text": "Cases Dekho ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_53_convex_lens_cases",
      "card_slug": "card_53_convex_lens_cases",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "convex_lens_image",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 53,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Convex Lens Cases: (1) Object at INFINITY → Image at F (opposite side), highly diminished, real, inverted. (2) Object BEYOND 2F → Image between F and 2F (opposite side), diminished, real, inverted (Camera principle!). (3) Object AT 2F → Image at 2F (opposite side), same size, real, inverted."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Aur focus pe rakhe toh?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "(4) Object BETWEEN F and 2F → Image beyond 2F (opposite side), magnified, real, inverted (Projector!). (5) Object AT F → Image at infinity, highly magnified, real, inverted. (6) Object BETWEEN O and F → Image on SAME side as object, magnified, virtual, erect (Magnifying glass!). ⭐ NEET/BPSC Tabular Question Favorite!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Aur concave lens mein?"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_54_concave_lens_image",
        "button_text": "Concave Lens ➔"
      }
    },
    {
      "card_id": "card_54_concave_lens_image",
      "card_slug": "card_54_concave_lens_image",
      "type": "chat",
      "concept_id": "concave_lens_image",
      "level": 5,
      "progress": {
        "current": 54,
        "total": 72
      },
      "messages": [
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Concave lens bahut simple - object ki position kuch bhi ho, image ALWAYS: (1) On SAME side as object (between O and F), (2) Virtual, (3) Erect, (4) Diminished. Exactly like convex mirror! ⭐ BPSC Statement Trap - 'Concave lens can form real image' is ALWAYS FALSE!"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Toh concave lens aur convex mirror dono ka behavior same hai?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "aman",
          "text": "Haan! Aur similarly, Convex lens aur Concave mirror dono converging devices hain - both can form real, magnified images depending on object position! Ek quick memory trick - CONVEX = CONVERGING (C-C), CONCAVE = DIVERGING (opposite)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "raju",
          "text": "Ye comparison helpful hai!"
        }
      ],
      "navigation": {
        "next": "card_55_quiz_lenses_1",
        "button_text": "Test Karo ➔"
      }
    },
    {
